          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-War Activism and Legacy</strong><br>\nAfter her tenure as First Lady, Eleanor remained active in public service, focusing on issues such as youth employment and education. Her legacy as an activist and advocate for justice and equality continued to inspire generations after her death on November 7, 1962.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Published Works and Communication</strong><br>\nEleanor was a prolific writer, authoring several books and articles on her life, experiences, and social issues. She communicated directly with the public through her daily syndicated newspaper column, \"My Day,\" which ran from 1935 to 1962.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Strength and Public Image</strong><br>\nDespite personal challenges, including her husband's polio and infidelity, Eleanor showed remarkable resilience. She maintained a robust public image and is remembered as one of the most influential women of the 20th century, leaving a profound impact on the role of women in American society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
